"""Original Codex manuscript: Rural Development."""
import sys
from pathlib import Path
TD=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;sys.path.insert(0,str(TD.parent))
from manuscript_support import build
E=[
('01-00','Livelihoods are not a ladder','Hunter-gathering obtains wild food, pastoralism keeps livestock, and agricultural livelihoods cultivate crops; households may combine activities.','These descriptions concern livelihoods, not a ranking of intelligence or progress. Movement, production and exchange vary between communities and over time.'),
('01-01','Hunter-gatherers','Hunter-gathering involves collecting wild plant foods and hunting or otherwise obtaining wild animal resources.','Knowledge of seasonal availability and habitats supports this livelihood. It does not imply isolation, lack of technology or an absence of trade.'),
('01-02','Nomadic pastoralists','Nomadic pastoralists move with livestock to access resources such as pasture and water.','Movement responds to herd needs and environmental conditions. This differs from remaining on cultivated fields; some households combine pastoral and crop activities.'),
('01-03','Agriculturalists','Agriculturalists cultivate crops, sometimes alongside livestock or other activities.','Cultivation commonly ties work to particular fields and seasonal tasks; this does not mean farmers never move or that every household follows the same arrangement.'),
('02-00','Rural society changes','Land access, work organisation, tools, seasons and social relationships interact in rural production.','Traditional practices are not timeless or uniform. Exchange, innovation and political changes can alter the resources and choices available to households.'),
('02-01','Land use and ownership','Land use describes activities on land, while ownership and access arrangements describe rights and control.','Community-managed access may support shared grazing; private or public arrangements have different decision structures. Identify actual rights rather than assuming communal means unrestricted.'),
('02-02','Division of labour','Division of labour splits productive work into specialised tasks carried out by different workers.','Household or community allocations can organise planting, herding, processing and care. Arrangements vary; do not portray gender or age roles as natural, fixed or identical everywhere.'),
('02-03','Tools','Tools apply human knowledge and effort to productive tasks, from hand hoes to animal-drawn equipment.','A tool’s value depends on the task, available skills, maintenance and conditions. Simple equipment can be suitable without being appropriate for every purpose.'),
('02-04','Seasonal agriculture','Seasonal agriculture schedules tasks such as preparation, planting and harvesting around relevant seasonal conditions.','For rain-fed crops, delayed rainfall can alter planting and labour demand. Storage and varied livelihoods can help manage a seasonal income or food gap.'),
('02-05','Social and cultural activity','Shared knowledge, values and social activities can organise cooperation, mutual support and resource decisions.','They can support collective work and adaptation, but members may have different interests. Consult affected people rather than assuming one speaker represents a whole community.'),
('02-06','Local environments','Soil, water, vegetation and climate shape productive opportunities and constraints.','Environmental knowledge can help match activities to local conditions. Dependence on rainfall or a narrow resource base can expose households to shocks without making outcomes inevitable.'),
('02-07','Pre-colonial changes','Farming before colonial rule could change through local innovation, exchange of knowledge, crops and tools, and shifts in settlement and trade.','Calling a system traditional does not mean it remained unchanged. Distinguish a process of adaptation from a claim that all communities adopted the same technique at one time.'),
('02-08','Colonial influences','Colonial rule could reshape land control, labour obligations and market relationships, often restricting local autonomy and access.','Loss of productive land or pressure to earn cash could change livelihoods and work allocation. Effects varied; explain the specific mechanism rather than treating one history as universal.'),
('02-09','Peasant farming and cash crops','Smallholder or peasant farming uses household-scale productive arrangements; cash cropping grows crops primarily for sale rather than direct household consumption.','A household may combine food and cash crops. Sales can finance needs, but price risk or reduced food-crop area can increase dependence on purchases.'),
('03-00','Commercial farming','Commercial farming produces primarily for sale and can use varied scales, technologies and crop arrangements.','It differs from subsistence farming primarily meeting household needs. Market orientation does not by itself establish large scale, monoculture or sustainable practice.'),
('03-01','Farming tools and technology','Equipment can assist planting, irrigation, harvesting or processing.','It can improve timeliness or capacity if suitable inputs, operators and repair support exist; an expensive unused machine does not raise useful production.'),
('03-02','Monoculture and scale','Monoculture is growing a single crop over an area, often repeatedly; distinguish it from rotating different crops.','Specialisation can simplify equipment and marketing but can concentrate pest, weather and price risks. Large scale and monoculture are separate features.'),
('03-03','Plantations','A plantation is an estate producing a commercial crop, often a perennial crop, organised for market supply.','Concentrated output can support processing and employment, but land access, labour conditions and dependence on one market affect the development result.'),
('03-04','Crop rotation','Crop rotation is growing different crops in a planned sequence on the same land over time.','A suitable sequence can help manage soil conditions and interrupt some pest cycles. It differs from repeating one crop or mixing several crops at the same time.'),
('04-00','Evaluate rural schemes','Rural schemes should address a specified livelihood or service barrier with feasible resources and participation.','Production obstacles stop growing or making output; marketing obstacles prevent reaching buyers or obtaining sales. A loan for seed will not repair a blocked selling route.'),
('04-01','Credit and small projects','Credit makes resources available now with repayment obligations; a small project may fit local skills and manageable demand.','It can remove a finance bottleneck, but repayment timing, borrowing cost and uncertain returns matter. Debt cannot by itself create water, markets or viable yields.'),
('04-02','Farmers’ cooperatives','A cooperative is owned and operated jointly by members to meet shared economic or social needs.','Pooling storage, purchases or marketing can improve access and bargaining, but requires agreed contributions, transparent decisions and workable coordination.'),
('04-03','Resettlement','Resettlement relocates people or productive access to another area under an organised scheme.','Land alone is insufficient: water, services, livelihoods, consent and continuing support matter. Moving people to unsuitable land can replace one access problem with another.'),
('04-04','Integrated development','Integrated rural development links related needs such as roads, water, skills, health and market access rather than treating them in isolation.','Community mobilisation can coordinate local knowledge and effort. Complex coordination and maintenance responsibilities can impede implementation if resources and roles are unclear.'),
('04-05','Grassroots development','Grassroots development is initiated with active participation by local people around their priorities and resources, rather than solely directed from above.','Local knowledge can improve relevance and ownership; unequal voice, limited funding or excluded groups can undermine those benefits. Participation needs influence, not attendance alone.'),
('04-06','Appropriate farming methods','Appropriate farming methods fit the soil, water, task, skills, costs and environmental conditions.','A method that conserves soil but cannot be maintained may fail in practice. Judge useful output and long-term resource condition together rather than choosing by complexity alone.'),
('05-00','Protect productive environments','Conservation protects the resources and environmental conditions supporting rural lives and production.','Immediate output gains from damaging soil, water or habitats can undermine future opportunities. Sustainable agriculture applies this wider development principle specifically to farming.'),
('05-01','Land clearance','Clearing vegetation can create growing space but can expose soil and remove habitats.','Where rain or wind acts on unprotected soil, loss of cover can increase erosion. Suitability depends on slope, remaining protection and the productive purpose.'),
('05-02','Fuelwood','Wood is renewable where regrowth replaces harvesting under suitable conditions.','Cutting faster than regeneration can deplete woodland, reduce future fuel access and expose soil. Efficient stoves and managed harvesting must fit users’ needs and maintenance capacity.'),
('05-03','Erosion and degradation','Erosion removes soil; land degradation reduces productive condition through processes such as erosion, loss of fertility or damaging use.','Reduced topsoil or vegetation can lower yields and increase vulnerability, so the response should match the cause rather than merely add more seed.'),
('05-04','Agricultural pollution','Agricultural chemicals or wastes can reach soil and water through inappropriate use, storage or runoff.','Where runoff carries pollutants into a shared source, downstream users and ecosystems may be affected. Safe management and suitable buffers address the pathway; more extraction does not.'),
('05-05','Wildlife diversity','Wildlife diversity includes different species and habitats that support ecological relationships and other values.','Habitat loss can reduce that diversity. Conservation should also consider local access, safety and livelihood costs rather than assuming benefits are shared equally.'),
('05-06','Sustainable rural development','Sustainable rural development meets present needs while protecting future capacity to meet needs.','An improvement can raise useful output by reducing waste rather than extracting more resources. Continued maintenance, inclusion and resource condition determine whether it lasts.'),
('06-00','Actors work together','The state, organised groups, informal networks and individuals can contribute different resources and responsibilities to rural development.','A shared problem needs clear roles and participation. A community committee cannot necessarily finance a regional network, while a distant agency may lack local information.'),
('06-01','The state','Public action can provide infrastructure, services and coordination across areas or groups.','Funding needs implementation, maintenance and accountability. An announced project is not evidence that services reach rural households.'),
('06-02','Formal groups','Formal groups have recognised structures such as membership rules, officers or recorded procedures.','They can pool resources and keep accountable records. Useful representation depends on whose voices influence decisions, not simply the group’s formal status.'),
('06-03','Informal groups','Informal groups coordinate through less formal relationships, such as neighbours sharing tasks.','Trust and local knowledge can support rapid cooperation; limited resources or unclear responsibilities may constrain larger or long-term work.'),
('06-04','Individuals','Individuals contribute work, knowledge, decisions and initiative within their actual opportunities.','An individual can maintain a local tool or share a technique, but wider finance or infrastructure constraints may require collective action. Avoid assigning structural failures solely to personal effort.'),
('06-05','Culture and values','Beliefs and values can influence goals, acceptable practices and willingness to participate.','Respectful consultation can identify both valued practices and desired changes. Culture is diverse and changing; it should not be used as a blanket explanation for poverty or resistance.'),
('07-00','Diversify with participation','Tourism can diversify rural livelihoods beyond a narrow productive base, while remaining dependent on demand and suitable access.','Local ownership, employment or supply links are needed for retained benefits. Diversification does not remove seasonality, environmental costs or the need for other livelihoods.'),
('07-01','Cultural tourism','Cultural tourism involves visitors’ interest in cultural knowledge, practices, heritage or activities.','Community control and consent can support income and valued heritage; pressure to perform, unequal returns or intrusion can harm participants. Judge benefits under terms people can influence.'),
('07-02','Conservation and participation','Community participation can connect conservation decisions with local knowledge, rights and benefits.','Conservation-related visitor income can support habitat protection where local owners, workers or suppliers retain benefits; restricted access or unequal returns can weaken support.'),
('08-00','Tourism has several effects','Tourism can create demand for local work and supplies while placing pressure on water, land, services and cultural life.','Trace the mechanism and who gains or bears costs. A visitor count alone cannot establish net local development.'),
('08-01','Effects on people','Tourism can provide jobs, skills and exchange, but can also produce seasonal work, rising local costs or unwanted intrusion.','Outcomes depend on participation, conditions and community influence. Avoid treating residents as a single group with identical gains.'),
('08-02','Economic effects','Tourist spending can support incomes and suppliers when local workers, owners and producers participate and retain earnings.','Imported supplies or profits sent elsewhere reduce some local links; uncertain demand and seasonality can make earnings unstable.'),
('08-03','Environmental effects','Tourism can finance conservation or create incentives to protect habitats, but can also increase waste, water demand and disturbance.','Visitor management, maintained facilities and meaningful local participation affect the balance. A protected-area label alone does not establish sustainable use.')]
Q={}
def q(n,p,a,g):Q[n]=(p,a,g)
q(1,'Identify the livelihood based on moving with herds to reach pasture and water.','Nomadic pastoralism.',['Feature: livestock-linked mobility.'])
q(2,'Differentiate between hunter-gathering and crop cultivation.','Hunter-gathering obtains wild resources; cultivation grows crops on managed land.',['Comparison: wild collection/hunting versus cultivation.'])
q(55,'Explain why people moving with livestock to seasonal pasture are not described simply as settled crop farmers.','Their movement follows herd resources; settled cultivation centres on fields. Test livestock-linked mobility rather than assuming all rural people have the same livelihood.',['Error: distinct livelihood arrangements merged.','Distinction: herd mobility versus field cultivation.'])
feature={3:('hunter-gathering','01-01'),4:('nomadic pastoralism','01-02'),5:('crop cultivation','01-03'),8:('land use and rights','02-01'),9:('rural division of labour','02-02'),10:('tools in rural production','02-03'),11:('seasonal agriculture','02-04'),12:('social cooperation in rural activity','02-05'),13:('dependence on local environmental conditions','02-06'),14:('change in farming before colonial rule','02-07'),15:('colonial influences on rural livelihoods','02-08'),16:('smallholder farming and cash crops','02-09'),19:('commercial farming equipment','03-01'),20:('monoculture and scale','03-02'),21:('plantations','03-03'),22:('crop rotation','03-04'),52:('tourism impacts on people','08-01'),53:('tourism economic links','08-02'),54:('tourism environmental effects','08-03')}
lookup={s:(m,e) for s,h,m,e in E}
for n,(term,s) in feature.items():q(n,'Describe two relevant characteristics of '+term+'.',' '.join(lookup[s]),['Feature: '+lookup[s][0], 'Feature: '+lookup[s][1]])
q(6,'State two interacting influences on rural production.','Seasonal water availability and the organisation of labour.',['Feature: two relevant influences; accept tools, access or social organisation.'])
q(7,'Explain the constraint in a fictional village where planting depends on seasonal rain but workers are absent when the rain arrives.','The useful planting window and available labour do not coincide. Delayed planting can limit crop establishment and expected output despite rain arriving.',['Mechanism: timing of labour relative to rainfall.','Consequence: restricted crop establishment/output.'])
q(17,'Identify the farming orientation when crops are grown primarily for sale.','Commercial farming.',['Feature: market orientation.'])
q(18,'Describe this fictional comparison: Farm A repeats one crop and receives all crop revenue from it; B rotates crops and earns revenue from three.','A concentrates crop and income exposure; B combines crop rotation with a more varied revenue base. A price fall in one crop therefore exposes a larger share of A’s recorded crop income.',['Evidence: use crop practice and revenue concentration.','Relationship: link concentration to exposure, without claiming rotation eliminates risk.'])
q(23,'Explain why a rural scheme should start with the actual production or marketing constraint.','Different obstacles require different responses: seed finance cannot restore a blocked delivery road. Identifying the mechanism directs resources towards the barrier to useful output or sales.',['Reason: match obstacle and response.'])
q(24,'Explain which suits a fictional village with produce and buyers but an impassable market road: seed loans or a funded road repair with maintenance. The aim is usable sales.','Repair is better aligned because output and demand exist but deliveries fail. Seed loans may add production while leaving the selling barrier; maintenance is needed to keep access usable.',['Judgement: prioritise sales access.','Reason: weigh both options using output, buyers and road evidence.'])
cases={25:('a seed loan','a household with suitable water, buyers and skills but no seed finance; repayment is due after harvest','The loan addresses the missing input and timing fits returns, but affordability and crop risk must be assessed; a grant or smaller trial may reduce debt exposure.'),26:('a cooperative store','growers needing shared storage, with agreed contributions but uneven decision-making influence','Pooling can improve access, but transparent participation and benefit rules are needed; sole individual storage may be unaffordable.'),27:('resettlement','productive land is offered far from water and markets, while improving water at the present site is feasible','Land alone does not make relocation viable. Present-site improvement is better supported unless water, livelihoods and access at the destination are secured.'),28:('an integrated scheme','a village has simultaneous water and road barriers; agencies can fund each but have no joint timetable','Coordinating both can remove interacting constraints, but responsibilities and sequencing must be agreed; an isolated road project leaves the water shortage.'),29:('a grassroots scheme','local priorities guide a funded project, but women and distant households are absent from decisions','Local leadership supports relevance but not yet inclusive influence. Broader consultation is needed; meeting attendance by one group is not full representation.'),30:('a locally repairable soil-cover method','soil erosion is observed, users have the materials and skills, and an alternative powered machine lacks repair support','The repairable method addresses erosion with available capabilities. The machine’s potential capacity is weaker evidence where maintenance is absent; check the method’s actual soil and yield effects.')}
for n,(option,case,answer) in cases.items():q(n,f'Explain whether {option} is suitable for lasting and inclusive rural improvement in this fictional case: {case}. Weigh the relevant benefit and constraint.',answer,['Judgement: decide against lasting, inclusive improvement.','Reason: '+answer])
q(56,'Explain why a farmer with a harvested crop and buyers but no usable delivery route has a marketing problem rather than simply a growing problem.','The crop has been produced; access to buyers prevents selling it. Test whether the barrier stops growing output or reaching and selling to the market.',['Error: sales-access obstacle treated as crop-growing failure.','Distinction: production versus marketing test.'])
q(31,'Explain why soil conservation matters to rural livelihoods.','Protecting productive soil preserves a resource needed for future yields, so immediate extraction that causes erosion may reduce later food or income.',['Reason: connect resource condition to future production.'])
chains={32:('05-00','an exposed cultivated slope loses topsoil after heavy rain and later crops grow poorly','Loss of protective cover lets runoff remove productive soil, reducing the growing base; restoring suitable cover addresses future production, rather than adding seed alone.'),33:('05-01','vegetation is cleared on a slope, then rain carries soil into the lower field','Clearance removes protection and runoff transports soil, reducing productive topsoil and affecting the lower field.'),34:('05-02','160 trees are cut annually while 100 replace losses under unchanged conditions','Harvesting exceeds regeneration by 60 trees each year, so fuel stock declines despite wood being renewable; continued overuse threatens future supply.'),35:('05-03','a field’s topsoil washes away and the same seed produces less under otherwise comparable conditions','Loss of productive soil can reduce crop performance; increasing seed does not restore the damaged growing base.'),36:('05-04','agricultural waste is left beside a drainage channel and rainfall carries it to a shared water source','Runoff transports the waste into the supply environment, creating a pollution pathway affecting downstream users; source management must address that route.'),37:('05-05','habitat clearance removes nesting sites and fewer birds are observed using the area','Loss of habitat can reduce use by affected species and biodiversity; the observation supports a habitat link without proving every species changed.'),38:('05-06','a storage repair reduces losses from 30 to 10 bags out of the same 100 harvested, with buyers available','Twenty more bags remain useful from unchanged harvesting. Reduced waste can support sales without more extraction, if storage maintenance continues.')}
for n,(s,case,a) in chains.items():q(n,'Explain the rural-development mechanism in this fictional case: '+case+'.',a,['Mechanism: connect the two supplied conditions.','Consequence: '+a])
q(39,'Explain why public agencies and local groups may need to coordinate a rural water project.','Agencies can supply network resources while residents contribute knowledge about access and use; clear roles join those capabilities and support maintenance.',['Reason: complementary resources linked to implementation.'])
q(40,'Suggest a practical arrangement for an invented village with a funded pump but no maintenance responsibility and residents reporting confusing operating times.','The provider and a representative local committee should agree a maintenance contact, operating timetable and fault-reporting arrangement. This addresses responsibility and information gaps using the existing funded asset.',['Context: funded pump, missing roles and timetable.','Reason: link clear arrangements to usable service.'])
for n,s in [(41,'06-01'),(42,'06-02'),(43,'06-03'),(44,'06-04'),(45,'06-05')]:
 q(n,'Explain one contribution or limitation of '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+' in rural development.',' '.join(lookup[s]),['Reason: '+lookup[s][1]])
q(46,'Explain which fictional tourism plan better fits locally retained livelihoods: A uses local guides and suppliers with agreed community control; B attracts more visitors but imports staff and supplies. Both have viable demand.','A has clearer local income links through employment, purchases and influence. B’s larger visitor count may raise total receipts but supplies weaker evidence of retained local benefits; assess costs and capacity too.',['Judgement: choose against retained local livelihoods.','Reason: weigh visitor volume against actual participation and supply links.'])
q(47,'Suggest a participation arrangement where a fictional village wants visitor income but residents are concerned about unwanted access to cultural activities.','Residents should agree which activities are offered, access limits, consent and how local guides or suppliers retain earnings. This links feasible income to community control rather than assuming access to private practices.',['Context: income aim and stated cultural concern.','Reason: match consent and retained benefits.'])
q(48,'Explain whether cultural tourism fits a fictional community with willing paid guides but disagreement over showing private ceremonies. A guided public-history walk is an alternative.','The public-history option is better supported while ceremony consent is unresolved. It can use willing guides and retain local earnings without assuming permission; visitor interest alone does not justify intrusion.',['Judgement: weigh income and consent.','Reason: compare the actual alternatives and participation conditions.'])
q(49,'Explain whether a fictional conservation visitor scheme suits local well-being when habitats are protected but grazing access is restricted and residents receive no work or supply contracts.','Habitat protection is a benefit, but livelihood costs and absent local income links weaken the proposal. Participation and workable benefit/access arrangements are needed; a conservation label alone does not resolve the trade-off.',['Judgement: conditional suitability for local well-being.','Reason: weigh protection with access costs and retained-benefit gaps.'])
q(50,'Explain the income mechanism where a fictional visitor centre buys local produce and hires local guides, while confirmed visitors can reach it.','Visitor demand creates paid work and sales for participating households, retaining some spending locally. The access and local supply/employment links make the gain plausible; visitor numbers alone would not establish it.',['Mechanism: visitor spending through local work and purchases.','Consequence: retained household income under supplied demand/access.'])
q(51,'Describe this original fictional tourism comparison: A retains N$60 of each N$100 visitor spending locally and uses 40 water units per visitor; B retains N$20 and uses 70.','A combines greater local retention with lower water demand: N$40 more retained and 30 fewer water units per visitor. The two measures favour A on those criteria, without a complete net-impact verdict.',['Evidence: accurate paired comparisons.','Relationship: link retention and resource demand against shared criteria.'])
P={
'P01':(['04-00','04-01'],'State (a) one production obstacle and one marketing obstacle [2]. Explain (b) why a seed loan may fail to improve an invented group’s sales when it already has stored crops and buyers but the market road is blocked [4].','(a) Insufficient growing water; a blocked route to buyers. (b) The constraint lies in selling existing output, not producing more. Seed credit adds debt and potential crops without restoring delivery; a feasible access response addresses the stated mechanism.',['Point: two correctly distinguished obstacles [2].','Development: use both existing output/buyers and blocked access, then explain why the loan misses the constraint [4].']),
'P02':(['05-00','05-06'],'Describe (a) this fictional comparison for 100 harvested bags: A loses 30 before sale; B loses 10, with the same available buyers [3]. Suggest (b) a priority if the aim is higher useful sales without more land clearance, and a feasible storage repair is available for A [3].','(a) B has 90 usable bags versus A’s 70, so lower storage loss accompanies 20 more useful bags from the same harvest. (b) Repair A’s storage and maintain it: reducing avoidable loss can improve sales without expanding cleared land. Buyers and repair feasibility make this a matched response.',['Evidence: compare loss and useful output [3].','Meaning: justify the storage priority against useful sales and unchanged land use [3].'])}
examples=[('01','Distinguish livelihoods','A fictional household moves its herd to seasonal pasture, while another remains near cultivated fields. The first description identifies nomadic pastoralism; the second describes cultivation. A household may combine activities, so the categories are tools for description rather than permanent identities.',['01-00','01-01','01-02','01-03']),('02','A seasonal constraint',Q[7][0]+' '+Q[7][1],['02-00','02-04']),('03','Read farm organisation',Q[18][0]+' '+Q[18][1],['03-00','03-02','03-04']),('04','Match the scheme',Q[24][0]+' '+Q[24][1],['04-00','04-01']),('05','Protect useful output',Q[38][0]+' '+Q[38][1],['05-00','05-06']),('06','Allocate responsibilities',Q[40][0]+' '+Q[40][1],['06-00','06-01','06-02','06-03','06-04','06-05']),('07','Evaluate retained gains',Q[46][0]+' '+Q[46][1],['07-00','07-01','07-02']),('08','Compare impacts',Q[51][0]+' '+Q[51][1],['08-00','08-01','08-02','08-03'])]
links={n:'Connect this lesson to Production (3.1), Sustainable Development (1.2) and Urbanisation (4.2). Identify the livelihood or resource constraint, trace the mechanism and distinguish a feasible action from a hoped-for outcome. The cases here are fictional, not claims about a named present-day community.' for n in ['01','02','03','04','05','06','07','08']}
if __name__=='__main__':build(TD,E,Q,P,links,examples)
